      "sample_index": 3,
      "text": "To determine which graphs of functions have inverses, we must analyze each graph to see if there are any points where the function is not one-to-one (i.e., the function is not strictly increasing or decreasing). If the function is one-to-one, then its inverse exists. If the function is not one-to-one, then its inverse does not exist.\n\nLet's analyze each graph:\n\n**Graph A:**\nThis graph is decreasing from left to right and then gradually flattens out until it is approximately a straight line. Since it is not strictly decreasing, we can conclude that it does not have an inverse.\n\n**Graph B:**\nThis graph looks like it passes the vertical line test, meaning it does not have any vertical gaps. However, it appears to be a parabola that opens upwards. Parabolas do not have inverses because they are not one-to-one over their entire domain.\n\n**Graph C:**\nThis graph looks like it passes the vertical line test. It appears to be a straight line that passes through the point (0,0) and another point. Since it is a straight line, it is one-to-one and thus has an inverse.\n\n**Graph D:**\nThis graph is cutting diagonally and there are intersection points indicating that it is not one-to-one over the entire domain. This means that for certain values of \\( x \\), there are two possible values of \\( y \\). As a result, the function does not have an inverse.\n\n**Graph E:**\nThis graph is a parabola that opens upwards. After approximately \\( x = 2 \\), it flattens out. Since parabolas are not one-to-one, this graph does not have an inverse.\n\nSo, the graphs that have inverses are C and E.\n\n\\(\\boxed{C, E}\\)",
